# Development Workflow

## Start of every task

1. Check Git state first.
   - `git status`
   - `git branch --show-current`
   - If the working tree is clean, run `git pull --ff-only`
   - Do not pull over uncommitted changes without reviewing them
2. Read `AGENTS.md`.
3. Read `README.md`.
4. Read `docs/00_project-overview.md` and `docs/05_current-status.md`.
5. Read related docs under `docs/01` to `docs/08`, features, decisions, and testing as needed.
6. Inspect the existing code and configuration before proposing changes.
7. Present an implementation plan before editing code or docs.
8. Do not edit until a human approves the plan.

## During implementation

- Do not change business rules without explicit approval.
- Do not guess missing specifications.
- Keep changes scoped to the approved feature or fix.
- Follow existing architecture, naming, formatting, and test conventions.
- Separate UI, business logic, and data access.
- Never commit secrets, credentials, personal data, or confidential production data.
- Do not hardcode PC-specific absolute paths. Use repository-relative paths or environment variables.
- Real absolute paths belong only in local `.env` or private notes, never in Git-managed files.

## Multi-PC and Pull Request policy

- Keep the GitHub repository structure identical across PCs.
- Do not edit the same branch on multiple PCs at the same time.
- Finish Commit and Push on the current PC before continuing on another PC.
- Integrate to main through a Pull Request after human review.
- Do not push directly to main without human approval.
- Do not force-push.

## Completion gate

Before Commit or Push, all of the following must be true:

- Implementation or documentation change is complete.
- Required tests pass, or are recorded as not applicable with reasons.
- Required build, type-check, and lint pass, or are recorded as not applicable with reasons.
- Acceptance criteria are satisfied.
- Relevant docs are updated.
- New architectural or business decisions are recorded.
- Git diff is reviewed.
- No secrets, confidential data, or real absolute paths are included.
- Human-required open questions are not left unresolved.

If any condition fails, do not Push.

## When tests or builds do not exist

- Do not invent or force test, lint, type-check, or build commands.
- Record those items as not applicable with reasons.
- For documentation-only or pre-implementation work, verify Markdown, links, structure, `git diff --check`, and absence of secrets.

## Required documentation updates

Update as applicable:

- `docs/05_current-status.md`
- `docs/06_development-setup.md`
- `docs/07_architecture.md`
- `docs/08_security-and-data-handling.md`
- `docs/changes/change-log.md`
- `docs/testing/`
- `docs/decisions/`

## Security and data handling

Follow `docs/08_security-and-data-handling.md`.

Do not commit:

- `.env`
- API keys, passwords, tokens, cookies, private keys
- real personal or customer data
- unprocessed production Excel/CSV
- confidential internal source files

## End-of-task report

Report:

- Changed files
- What was done
- Test, type-check, lint, and build results
- Items marked not applicable and why
- Updated docs
- Open issues
- Commit hash and Push branch when applicable
- Whether a Pull Request is needed
